{
  "title": "{cpa:Avrei voluto} potergliene parlare",
  "translation": "I would have liked to be able to talk to him about it",
  "topics": "Choosing and placing pronouns in longer verb constructions",
  "grammar": {
    "explain": [
      {
        "h": "Pronouns that climb"
      },
      "In a chain of verbs, pronouns may stay on the last infinitive or move up in front of the first verb. Both are correct, and the second one is often more natural in speech.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "On the infinitive",
            "Moved up"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "{ip:Voglio} parlargliene.",
              "Gliene {ip:voglio} parlare."
            ],
            [
              "{cpa:Avrei voluto} potergliene parlare.",
              "Gliene {cpa:avrei voluto} poter parlare."
            ],
            [
              "{ip:Vado} a prenderlo.",
              "Lo {ip:vado} a prendere."
            ],
            [
              "{ip:Sto} per dirtelo.",
              "Te lo {ip:sto} per dire."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "h": "When they cannot climb"
      },
      "Pronouns can move up only with modal verbs, verbs of motion, and a few aspect verbs such as *stare per*, *cominciare a*, *finire di* and *continuare a*. After other verbs followed by *di*, they must stay where they are: *{ip:Cerco} di dirtelo*, never *Te lo {ip:cerco} di dire*.",
      {
        "h": "Climbing can change the auxiliary"
      },
      "A modal verb takes the auxiliary of the infinitive that follows it. If a pronoun has moved up in front of it, *essere* becomes necessary with verbs that require it.",
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Without moving",
            "Moved up"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "{pp:Ho dovuto} alzarmi presto.",
              "Mi {pp:sono dovuto} alzare presto."
            ],
            [
              "{pp:Ho voluto} andarci.",
              "Ci {pp:sono voluto} andare."
            ],
            [
              "{pp:Ho potuto} parlargli.",
              "Gli {pp:ho potuto} parlare."
            ]
          ]
        }
      },
      "The third example keeps *avere*, because *parlare* takes *avere*.",
      {
        "h": "A neutral lo"
      },
      "*Lo* can replace a whole idea or an adjective: *{ip:Sei} stanco? Lo {ip:sono}.* *Bella lo {ip:è}, ma…* In speech, it often anticipates a clause: *Lo {im:sapevo} che {cpa:saresti venuto}*.",
      {
        "h": "Pronouns with impersonal si"
      },
      {
        "table": {
          "head": [
            "Combination",
            "Example"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "lo + si",
              "Lo si {ip:dice} spesso."
            ],
            [
              "si + ne",
              "Se ne {ip:parla}."
            ],
            [
              "ci + si (reflexive)",
              "Ci si {ip:lava}."
            ],
            [
              "ci + si (place)",
              "Ci si {ip:va} in treno."
            ],
            [
              "ci + si + ne",
              "Ci se ne {ip:accorge} subito."
            ]
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
